  "prediction": "The report outlines the constitutional process for Congress to override a presidential veto on a bill. When the President vetoes a bill, the chamber that originated it must enter the President's reasons for the veto in its Journal and then proceed to reconsider the bill. To become law, two-thirds of the Members in each chamber must agree to repass the bill and override the President's veto. The chamber of origin acts first on the question of reconsideration, with the House acting on bills with \"H.R.\" or \"H.J. Res.\" designations and the Senate acting on bills with \"S.\" or \"S.J. Res.\" designations. If the chamber of origin fails to vote on the question of repassing the bill, it dies at the end of the Congress. The Constitution does not require a direct vote on the question of repassing a vetoed bill, and chambers have the flexibility to refer the bill to committee instead. Debate on the question of overriding a veto is a matter of high privilege in the House and generally occurs shortly after the message is received. The House can agree to postpone consideration of a veto message or refer it to committee, and debate takes place under the hour rule. In the Senate, the message is typically held at the desk for several days before consideration, and debate is limited by unanimous consent or through the use of cloture. To override a veto, two-thirds of the Members in each chamber must agree, and the vote must be by the \"yeas and nays.\" If the override vote is unsuccessful, the chamber informs the other chamber and typically refers the bill and veto message to committee. If the House successfully overrides a Senate veto, the bill becomes law. If the Senate wishes to reconsider a vetoed bill, it can do so under the terms of a unanimous consent agreement or by a numerical majority through a nondebatable motion to proceed. The question of overriding a veto is debatable under the regular rules of the Senate and can be limited by unanimous consent or cloture.",
  "scored_prediction": "The report outlines the constitutional process for Congress to override a presidential veto on a bill. When the President vetoes a bill, the chamber that originated it must enter the President's reasons for the veto in its Journal and then proceed to reconsider the bill. To become law, two-thirds of the Members in each chamber must agree to repass the bill and override the President's veto. The chamber of origin acts first on the question of reconsideration, with the House acting on bills with \"H.R.\" or \"H.J. Res.\" designations and the Senate acting on bills with \"S.\" or \"S.J. Res.\" designations. If the chamber of origin fails to vote on the question of repassing the bill, it dies at the end of the Congress. The Constitution does not require a direct vote on the question of repassing a vetoed bill, and chambers have the flexibility to refer the bill to committee instead. Debate on the question of overriding a veto is a matter of high privilege in the House and generally occurs shortly after the message is received. The House can agree to postpone consideration of a veto message or refer it to committee, and debate takes place under the hour rule. In the Senate, the message is typically held at the desk for several days before consideration, and debate is limited by unanimous consent or through the use of cloture. To override a veto, two-thirds of the Members in each chamber must agree, and the vote must be by the \"yeas and nays.\" If the override vote is unsuccessful, the chamber informs the other chamber and typically refers the bill and veto message to committee. If the House successfully overrides a Senate veto, the bill becomes law. If the Senate wishes to reconsider a vetoed bill, it can do so under the terms of a unanimous consent agreement or by a numerical majority through a nondebatable motion to proceed. The question of overriding a veto is debatable under the regular rules of the Senate and can be limited by unanimous consent or cloture.",
  "score": 38.42794712923095,
  "answers": [
    "A bill or joint resolution that has been vetoed by the President can become law if two-thirds of the Members voting in the House and the Senate each agree to pass it over the President's objection. The chambers act sequentially on vetoed measures: The House acts first on House-originated measures (H.R. and H.J. Res.), and the Senate acts first on Senate-originated measures (S. and S.J. Res.). If the first-acting chamber fails to override the veto, the other chamber cannot consider it. The House typically considers the question of overriding a presidential veto under the hour rule, with time customarily controlled and allocated by the chair and ranking member of the committee with jurisdiction over the bill. The Senate usually considers the question of overriding a veto under the terms of a unanimous consent agreement."
  ],
